<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">James the Great</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 44 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a> · Also known as James, son of Zebedee, Saint James the Great</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">James the Great, son of Zebedee, was one of the twelve apostles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>, executed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herod-agrippa-i">Herod Agrippa I</a> around 44 CE. Medieval tradition held that his body had been carried to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>, and the discovery of his supposed tomb at Compostela in the early 9th century, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-ii-of-asturias">Alfonso II of Asturias</a>, created one of Christendom's great pilgrimage centres and made him patron of Christian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/james-the-great.jpg" alt="James the Great" width="320" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Peter Paul Rubens · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Peter_Paul_Rubens_-_St_James_the_Apostle_-_WGA20192.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>James was born into a family of Jewish fishermen on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-galilee">Sea of Galilee</a>. His parents were Zebedee and Salome, and Salome was a sister of Mary, the mother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>, which made James a cousin of Jesus. He was the brother of John the Apostle. He is called the Greater to distinguish him from the other apostle James, the Lesser, the term meaning older or taller rather than more important. The Synoptic Gospels state that James and John were preparing to fish with their father by the shore when Jesus called them to follow him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>James, John and Peter formed an informal triumvirate among the Twelve. They were the only apostles present at three occasions of the ministry of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>: the raising of Jairus' daughter, the transfiguration and the agony in the Garden of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gethsemane">Gethsemane</a>. The brothers were nicknamed Boanerges, the Sons of Thunder. James and John, or in another tradition their mother, asked Jesus for seats on his right and left in his glory, and Jesus rebuked them.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acts-of-the-apostles">Acts of the Apostles</a> records that Herod the king, usually identified with Herod Agrippa, had James executed by the sword. He was the first of the apostles to be martyred. The Armenian Apostolic Cathedral of St. James in the Armenian Quarter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> is the traditional site, and his head is believed to lie under the altar of its Chapel of Saint James the Great.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The Historia Compostelana, commissioned by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-gelmirez">Diego Gelmírez</a> in the 12th century, sets out the legend as it was believed at Compostela. James preached in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a> as well as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-land">Holy Land</a>, and after his execution his followers carried his body by sea to Padrón in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> and overland to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago-de-compostela">Santiago de Compostela</a>. The Compostela tradition places the discovery of the relics in the 9th century, when Pelayo found them in the Libredon forest in the time of Bishop Theodemir and King Alfonso II. The pilgrimage route known as the Way of St. James began to form from that century.</p><p>A later tradition has him appear in battle for the Christian army at Clavijo, as Santiago Matamoros, and Spanish Christian armies took his name as a battle cry. The military <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/order-of-santiago">Order of Santiago</a> was founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in the 12th century. His emblem is the scallop shell, which pilgrims wore, and his feast day is 25 July.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “James the Great” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…on the Apocalypse around 776 and attacked the Adoptionist teaching of Archbishop Elipandus of Toledo, and the discovery of what was believed to be the tomb of <b>Saint James</b> the Apostle at Compostela in the early ninth century began a cult that drew pilgrims from across Latin Europe.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-asturias">Kingdom of Asturias · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Bethsaida, in what is now <a href="/countries/israel">Israel</a>. Died in Jerusalem, in what is now <a href="/countries/palestine">Palestine</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=32.91,35.63&amp;year=34" aria-label="Bethsaida on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5463 968 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of James the Great"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5913 1238h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5913 1238h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=32.91,35.63&amp;year=34">What was here in 34 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
